                            {
                                "target_word": "rip",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "154ad86b-380b-3df3-882a-434f90924a6f",
                                        "entry_id": "93939675-b497-3a90-8e2c-5ea630dba338",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A tear (in paper, etc.)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "aa21c6ed-b1e7-3dc0-9f86-7138005d9257",
                                        "entry_id": "93939675-b497-3a90-8e2c-5ea630dba338",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A type of strong, rough tide or current.",
                                            "A rip current: a strong outflow of surface water, away from the shore, that returns water from incoming waves."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Australia",
                                            "New-Zealand"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "118b0089-8041-4014-b6a7-2a26e8724368",
                                                "sense_id": "aa21c6ed-b1e7-3dc0-9f86-7138005d9257",
                                                "text": "Rhythmic beaches consist of a rhythmic longshore bar that narrows and deepens when the rip crosses the breaker, and in between broadens, shoals and approaches the shore. It does not, however, reach the shore, with a continuous rip feeder channel feeding the rips to either side of the bar."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "fafdc8c2-de1d-493b-9f16-c28b51a75f68",
                                                "sense_id": "aa21c6ed-b1e7-3dc0-9f86-7138005d9257",
                                                "text": "Undertows (or ‘rips’) are the main problem. If you find yourself being carried out by a rip, the important thing to do is just keep afloat; don′t panic or try to swim against the rip, which will exhaust you. In most cases the current stops within a couple of hundred metres of the shore and you can then swim parallel to the shore for a short way to get out of the rip and make your way back to land."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "53ef4637-ac42-452e-9eb7-c407b92649ba",
                                                "sense_id": "aa21c6ed-b1e7-3dc0-9f86-7138005d9257",
                                                "text": "2010, Jeff Wilks, Donna Prendergast, Chapter 9: Beach Safety and Millennium Youth: Travellers and Sentinels, Pierre Benckendorff, Gianna Moscardo, Donna Pendergast, Tourism and Generation Y, page 100,\nGiven that a large number of all rescues conducted by Surf Life Saving Australia (SLSA) occur in rips (a rip being a relatively narrow, seaward moving stream of water), this is critical surf-safety information (Surf Life Saving Australia, 2005)."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "4131230f-2172-3947-94ee-e225162fdaf6",
                                        "entry_id": "93939675-b497-3a90-8e2c-5ea630dba338",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A type of strong, rough tide or current.",
                                            "A tract of broken water (in a river or stream), particularly one which is not as rough as rapids."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "in-plural"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "db2cd77d-0b95-47a5-b820-319f6a4e057b",
                                                "sense_id": "4131230f-2172-3947-94ee-e225162fdaf6",
                                                "text": "At rare intervals the water is smooth and deep, but the rips, rapids and falls give the river its distinctive character. About two and a half miles from its mouth it contracts somewhat, and plunges over a precipice one hundred feet high[…]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "09275712-3fd4-4aa4-be05-d3851467f63f",
                                                "sense_id": "4131230f-2172-3947-94ee-e225162fdaf6",
                                                "text": "In the 5.8 miles between this point and the head of Burnt Land Rips the fall is 40 feet. The East Branch joins the main river about 1 1⁄2 miles below the rips at Medway. A part of this fall could probably be developed by a dam near the head of the Joe Mary Rapids, but the pondage created by a high dam would cause damage[…]"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a6ead0cb-3001-46e2-89ea-e81175feb275",
                                                "sense_id": "4131230f-2172-3947-94ee-e225162fdaf6",
                                                "text": "At the outlet of Round Pond is the beginning of Round Pond Rips. Rips is a Maine word that generally means easy rapids. They are not hard rapids, but require your attention, as there are plenty of rocks and ledges that need to be avoided. After the rips the river is easy […]"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "6e78ae01-c169-312d-9507-c3590cd496f3",
                                        "entry_id": "93939675-b497-3a90-8e2c-5ea630dba338",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A comical, embarrassing, or hypocritical event or action."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "slang"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "eef1edd9-5f8c-39d1-a719-a7c3420cbaac",
                                        "entry_id": "93939675-b497-3a90-8e2c-5ea630dba338",
                                        "glosses": [
